\begin{abstract}
The pigeonhole principle bounds \emph{exact}, universal compression: an injective encoder into bitstrings of length at most $t$ can serve at most $2^{t+1}-1$ messages. We study the relaxation in which the decoder may fail with probability at most $\varepsilon$, subject to the strict requirement that it \emph{never fail silently} --- modelled by a partial decoder whose only alternative to the correct answer is an explicit failure symbol. We prove four groups of results. \emph{(i) Rate.} A sound code with codewords of length at most $t$ and failure probability at most $\varepsilon$ exists if and only if the source possesses a set $S$ of mass at least $1-\varepsilon$ with $|S|+2 \le 2^{t+1}$, or $S$ is the whole alphabet and $|S|+1 \le 2^{t+1}$; hence the $\varepsilon$-relaxation is exactly the $(1-\varepsilon)$-quantile of the source, and error detection costs exactly one codeword. For the uniform source, $(1-\varepsilon)N \le 2^{t+1}$. \emph{(ii) Randomness.} A uniformly random codebook on $q$ messages with $m$ slots collides with probability at most $q(q-1)/(2m)$, tight at $q=2$; and whenever a Monte Carlo codebook of rate $k$ succeeds, the deterministic enumerative code on the same typical set matches its rate and failure probability and decodes exponentially faster. \emph{(iii) Complexity.} Enumerative decoding costs exactly $k+2$ steps, whereas exhaustive search over an unstructured codebook of $n$ entries costs exactly $n$ probes in the worst case for \emph{every} storage order and $n(n+1)/2$ summed over all messages; the speed-up is unbounded. \emph{(iv) Robustness and composition.} A one-bit parity checksum detects every single-bit channel corruption at no cost in reliability, giving total decoding cost $2k+4$; and because the scheme is fixed rate, $n$ codewords concatenate parse-free with failure probability $1-(1-\varepsilon_0)^n \le n\varepsilon$ and decoding cost exactly $n(k+3)+1$ steps.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The immovable bound}

Let $\A$ be a finite set of messages, $|\A| = N$. A lossless compressor mapping every message to a bitstring of length at most $t$ must be injective, and there are exactly $2^{t+1}-1$ bitstrings of length at most $t$; hence $N \le 2^{t+1}-1$. This \emph{pigeonhole bound} is a counting statement, so no algorithmic advance can weaken it.

Practical compression evades it by changing the contract: real compressors need only work on messages that actually occur. This paper asks precisely what that change of contract buys.

\subsection{The relaxed contract, and the one thing we refuse to give up}

We relax ``always correct'' to ``correct with probability at least $1-\varepsilon$'', but we do \emph{not} relax correctness-when-answering. A decoder returning a wrong message is a \emph{silent corruption}; a decoder returning ``I failed'' is a detected failure. Only the second is permitted, formalised by making the decoder partial with codomain $\A \cup \{\bot\}$. Every scheme below comes with a failure-probability bound, an exact decoding-complexity figure, and a proof that failures are reported.

\subsection{The question of randomness}

Shannon's random-coding argument builds good codes by drawing them at random. Does randomness help under the relaxed contract? On \emph{rate}, no: a random codebook that succeeds is one that is injective on the typical set, and injectivity of $q$ messages into $m$ slots forces $q \le m$ --- exactly the counting constraint the deterministic scheme obeys. On \emph{complexity}, worse than no: an unstructured table admits only search, costing $\Theta(2^k)$ probes against $k+2$ steps.

\section{Definitions}

Throughout, $\A$ is a finite non-empty set, $\{0,1\}^*$ the finite bitstrings, and $p : \A \to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ a probability distribution with $\sum_{x} p(x) = 1$; we write $p(S) = \sum_{x \in S} p(x)$.

\begin{definition}[Code]
A \emph{code} on $\A$ is a pair $c = (E,D)$ with encoder $E : \A \to \{0,1\}^*$ and partial decoder $D : \{0,1\}^* \to \A \cup \{\bot\}$, where $\bot$ denotes an explicit, detected failure.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Soundness: no silent corruption]
$c$ is \emph{sound} if for all $x,y \in \A$, $D(E(x)) = y$ implies $y = x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Good set, failure probability]
$G(c) = \{x \in \A : D(E(x)) = x\}$ and $\fail_p(c) = p(\A \setminus G(c)) = 1 - p(G(c))$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Length bound, fixed rate]
$c$ has \emph{length bound} $t$ if $|E(x)| \le t$ for all $x$, and is \emph{fixed rate} $\ell$ if $|E(x)| = \ell$ for all $x$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[$\varepsilon$-almost-lossless]
$c$ is \emph{$\varepsilon$-almost-lossless for $p$} if it is sound and $\fail_p(c) \le \varepsilon$.
\end{definition}

Soundness constrains the decoder only on legitimately produced codewords; channel corruption is treated separately in Section~\ref{sec:checksum}.

\section{The converse: the $\varepsilon$-relaxed counting bound}

\begin{lemma}[Injectivity on the good set]\label{lem:injon}
If $c = (E,D)$ is sound then $E$ is injective on $G(c)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Let $x,y \in G(c)$ with $E(x) = E(y)$. Then $D(E(x)) = D(E(y)) = y$, and soundness gives $y = x$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Restricted pigeonhole]\label{lem:restricted}
If $c$ is sound with length bound $t$ then $|G(c)| + 1 \le 2^{t+1}$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:injon}, $E$ injects $G(c)$ into the $2^{t+1}-1$ bitstrings of length at most $t$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Mass accounting]\label{lem:mass}
For any code $c$, $p(G(c)) = 1 - \fail_p(c)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[$\varepsilon$-relaxed counting bound]\label{thm:relaxed}
If $c$ is sound with length bound $t$ and $\fail_p(c) \le \varepsilon$, there exists $S \subseteq \A$ with $|S| + 1 \le 2^{t+1}$ and $p(S) \ge 1-\varepsilon$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $S = G(c)$ and apply Lemmas~\ref{lem:restricted} and~\ref{lem:mass}.
\end{proof}

The relaxation creates no rate out of nothing: it replaces ``how many messages are there?'' by ``how many messages cover mass $1-\varepsilon$?'', the $(1-\varepsilon)$-quantile of the source.

\begin{corollary}[Falsifiability form]
If every $S$ with $|S|+1 \le 2^{t+1}$ has $p(S) < 1-\varepsilon$, then every sound code with length bound $t$ has $\fail_p(c) > \varepsilon$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform source]\label{thm:uniform}
Let $p$ be uniform on $\A$ with $N = |\A| \ge 1$. If $c$ is sound with length bound $t$ and $\fail_p(c) \le \varepsilon$, then $(1-\varepsilon)N \le 2^{t+1}$, and if $\varepsilon < 1$ also $\log_2((1-\varepsilon)N) \le t+1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:relaxed} yields $S$ with $|S|/N \ge 1-\varepsilon$ and $|S|+1 \le 2^{t+1}$; combine and take logarithms.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Conservativity at $\varepsilon = 0$]
A code that never fails on the uniform source satisfies $N \le 2^{t+1}$: the classical bound is recovered exactly.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}
The saving is at most $\log_2\frac{1}{1-\varepsilon}$ bits: $0.0145$ bits at $\varepsilon = 1\%$, $0.152$ at $\varepsilon = 10\%$, one bit only at $\varepsilon = 50\%$. Real gains come from non-uniformity, where the $(1-\varepsilon)$-quantile is exponentially smaller than $N$.
\end{remark}

\section{Achievability: the enumerative scheme}

Fix $S \subseteq \A$ and $k$ with $|S| \le 2^k$, and a bijection $\iota : S \to \{0,\dots,|S|-1\}$.

\begin{definition}[Binary index codec]
$\toBits(0,n)$ is the empty string and $\toBits(k+1,n) = [n \bmod 2] \cdot \toBits(k,\lfloor n/2\rfloor)$; $\fromBits(\,) = 0$ and $\fromBits(b \cdot l) = b + 2\fromBits(l)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:codec}
$|\toBits(k,n)| = k$, and $\fromBits(\toBits(k,n)) = n$ whenever $n < 2^k$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$, using $n = (n \bmod 2) + 2\lfloor n/2\rfloor$ and $\lfloor n/2 \rfloor < 2^k$ for $n < 2^{k+1}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Enumerative code $\Enum(S,k)$]
\[
E(x) = \begin{cases} 1 \cdot \toBits(k,\iota(x)), & x \in S,\\ 0 \cdot \toBits(k,0), & x \notin S,\end{cases}
\qquad
D(w) = \begin{cases} \iota^{-1}(\fromBits(r)), & w = 1\cdot r,\ \fromBits(r) < |S|,\\ \bot, & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Properties of the enumerative code]\label{thm:enum}
Assume $|S| \le 2^k$. Then \textup{(1)} $|E(x)| = k+1$ for every $x$ (fixed rate); \textup{(2)} $D(E(x)) = x$ for $x \in S$; \textup{(3)} $D(E(x)) = \bot$ for $x \notin S$; \textup{(4)} the code is sound; \textup{(5)} $G(\Enum(S,k)) = S$; \textup{(6)} $\fail_p(\Enum(S,k)) = p(\A \setminus S)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
(1) follows from Lemma~\ref{lem:codec}: both branches emit one flag bit and $k$ index bits. (2) $\iota(x) < |S| \le 2^k$, so the codec round-trips and the table access returns $x$. (3) the flag bit is $0$ and $D$ rejects such words. (4) is (2) and (3) together, (5) is their combination, and (6) follows from the definition of $\fail_p$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Achievability]\label{thm:achiev}
If $|S| \le 2^k$ and $p(S) \ge 1-\varepsilon$, then $\Enum(S,k)$ is sound, has length bound $k+1$, satisfies $\fail_p \le \varepsilon$, has good set $S$, and returns $\bot$ on every $x \notin S$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Uniform achievability]
For uniform $p$ and $2^k \le N$, taking $|S| = 2^k$ gives a sound code with length bound $k+1$ and failure probability exactly $1 - 2^k/N$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The exact rate, and the price of error detection}

\begin{theorem}[Price of error detection]\label{thm:price}
If $c$ is sound with length bound $t$ and $G(c) \ne \A$, then $|G(c)| + 2 \le 2^{t+1}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Lemma~\ref{lem:injon} gives $|G(c)| \le 2^{t+1}-1$. Suppose equality; then $E$ maps $G(c)$ bijectively onto all bitstrings of length at most $t$. Pick $x \notin G(c)$. Since $|E(x)| \le t$, $E(x) = E(y)$ for some $y \in G(c)$, so $D(E(x)) = y \ne x$, contradicting soundness.
\end{proof}

The extra unit is the codeword reserved for $\bot$: the decoder sees only the received string, so a detectable failure must occupy a string of its own.

\begin{theorem}[Realisable good sets]\label{thm:realisable}
For $S \subsetneq \A$: a sound code with length bound $t$ and good set exactly $S$ exists if and only if $|S| + 2 \le 2^{t+1}$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Theorem~\ref{thm:price}. ($\Leftarrow$) Choose an injection $\sigma$ of $S$ into the short strings missing at least one short string $w_\bot$; encode $x \in S$ as $\sigma(x)$ and every other $x$ as $w_\bot$, and decode $w$ to $\sigma^{-1}(w)$ when defined and to $\bot$ otherwise.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact optimal $\varepsilon$-almost-lossless rate]\label{thm:optimal}
For any $p$, $t$, $\varepsilon$: a sound code with length bound $t$ and $\fail_p \le \varepsilon$ exists if and only if there is $S \subseteq \A$ with $p(S) \ge 1-\varepsilon$ and
\[
\big(S = \A \ \text{and}\ |S| + 1 \le 2^{t+1}\big) \quad\text{or}\quad |S| + 2 \le 2^{t+1}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Take $S = G(c)$, using Lemma~\ref{lem:restricted} if $G(c) = \A$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:price} otherwise, together with Lemma~\ref{lem:mass}. ($\Leftarrow$) In the total case inject $\A$ into the short strings and decode by inversion; in the partial case apply Theorem~\ref{thm:realisable}. In both cases $\fail_p = 1 - p(S) \le \varepsilon$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The explicit scheme is within two bits]
If $|S| + 2 \le 2^{t+1}$ then $\Enum(S,t+1)$ is sound with length bound $t+2$ and good set exactly $S$: two bits of redundancy buy an exponentially faster decoder.
\end{corollary}

\section{Random codebooks: the birthday bound and its derandomisation}

A \emph{codebook} on $q$ messages with $m$ slots is a map $f : \{1,\dots,q\} \to \{1,\dots,m\}$; it \emph{works} exactly when injective. Draw $f$ uniformly from the $m^q$ possibilities.

\begin{definition}
$\Bad(q,m)$ is the set of non-injective maps $\{1,\dots,q\} \to \{1,\dots,m\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Exact count]\label{lem:count}
$|\Bad(q,m)| + m^{\underline{q}} = m^q$, where $m^{\underline q} = m(m-1)\cdots(m-q+1)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Birthday bound]\label{thm:birthday}
$2\,|\Bad(q,m)| \le q(q-1)m^{q-1}$; hence for $m \ge 1$,
\[
\Pr_f[\,f \text{ collides}\,] = \frac{|\Bad(q,m)|}{m^q} \le \frac{q(q-1)}{2m}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Lemma~\ref{lem:count} it suffices to show $2m^{q+1} \le 2m^{\underline{q+1}} + (q+1)q\,m^q$, which follows by induction on $q$ from $m^{\underline{q+1}} = (m-q)m^{\underline q}$ and $m^{\underline q} \le m^q$. Divide by $m^q$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Tightness]
$|\Bad(2,m)| = m$, so the collision probability at $q = 2$ is exactly $1/m = \frac{2\cdot 1}{2m}$: Theorem~\ref{thm:birthday} holds with equality and cannot be improved in general.
\end{theorem}

\begin{corollary}[Derandomisation]
If $q(q-1) < 2m$ the collision probability is below $1$, so an injective codebook exists.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Randomness buys no rate]\label{thm:norate}
If $f : \A \to \{1,\dots,m\}$ is injective on $S \subseteq \A$, then $|S| \le m$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Derandomisation of Monte Carlo compression]\label{thm:derand}
Suppose a Monte Carlo experiment produces $f : \A \to \{0,\dots,2^k-1\}$ injective on a typical set $S$ with $p(S) \ge 1-\varepsilon$. Then $\Enum(S,k)$ is sound, has length bound $k+1$, satisfies $\fail_p \le \varepsilon$, returns $\bot$ explicitly on every $x \notin S$, and decodes every $x \in S$ in exactly $k+2$ steps --- whereas decoding the random codebook by exhaustive search costs up to $2^k$ probes.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:norate} gives $|S| \le 2^k$, the hypothesis of Theorems~\ref{thm:enum} and~\ref{thm:enumcost}; the reliability bound is Lemma~\ref{lem:mass} with $G = S$.
\end{proof}

Random coding is a proof technique, not a compression resource: what it establishes is exactly $|S| \le 2^k$, which is already the design rule of the deterministic code.

\section{Exact decoder complexity and the separation}

We use \emph{instrumented} decoders returning both an answer and a step count; each is proved to compute the same function as the decoder it instruments, so the counts describe genuine decoding.

\begin{definition}[Instrumented enumerative decoder]
On input $w$: if $w$ is empty or begins with $0$, return $(\bot,1)$. Otherwise write $w = 1 \cdot r$, read $r$ with a bit-by-bit reader costing $|r|+1$ steps, perform one indexed table access, and charge one further step.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact enumerative decoding cost]\label{thm:enumcost}
If $|S| \le 2^k$ and $x \in S$, the instrumented enumerative decoder on $E(x)$ returns $x$ at a cost of exactly $k+2$ steps, and never exceeds $k+3$ on any codeword of the scheme.
\end{theorem}

\begin{definition}[Instrumented codebook scan]
Given a codebook $\cb$, a received word $w$ and a candidate list $l$, probe candidates left to right at one step per probe and stop at the first $a$ with $\cb(a) = w$; return $(\bot,|l|)$ if none matches.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Exact worst-case search cost]\label{thm:scanworst}
If the true message is the last entry of the candidate list and no earlier entry shares its codeword, the scan costs exactly the list length. For an injective codebook of $2^k$ entries some message costs exactly $2^k$ probes.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Worst case is order-independent]
For an injective codebook stored in any order as a duplicate-free non-empty list $l$, there exists $x \in l$ whose decoding costs exactly $|l|$ probes.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Take $x$ to be the last element and apply Theorem~\ref{thm:scanworst}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The average case is exponential too]
For an injective codebook stored as a duplicate-free list of length $n$, $\sum_{x} \mathrm{cost}(x) = n(n+1)/2$, an average of $(n+1)/2 \approx 2^{k-1}$ probes at rate $k$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction: the head costs $1$ and every other element costs one more than in the tail, so $T(n) = T(n-1) + n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}\label{lem:linexp}
$k+3 < 2^k$ for all $k \ge 4$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Decoder complexity separation]
For every rate $k \ge 4$, every $S$ with $|S| \le 2^k$ and every $x \in S$, the enumerative decoder's cost $k+2$ is strictly less than the $2^k$ probes of exhaustive search over an injective codebook of $2^k$ entries.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded speed-up]
For every $M \in \mathbb{N}$ there is a rate $k \ge 4$ with $M(k+3) < 2^k$; explicitly $k = 4M+8$ works.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$M(4M+11) \le (2M+4)^2 < 2^{4M+8}$, the last step from $2M+4 < 2^{2M+4}$ squared.
\end{proof}

Reordering the codebook does not help and the exponential cost is typical rather than exceptional. The enumerative code is fast for a structural reason: its codeword \emph{is} the address of the message in the typical-set table.

\section{Channel errors: a one-bit checksum keeps failures loud}\label{sec:checksum}

\begin{definition}[Parity]
$\pi(w) = w_1 \oplus \cdots \oplus w_{|w|}$, with $\pi(\text{empty}) = 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Checksummed code]
Given $c = (E,D)$, set $E^\pi(x) = E(x)\cdot \pi(E(x))$ and $D^\pi(w) = \bot$ if $\pi(w) = 1$, else $D$ applied to $w$ with its last bit removed.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
$\pi(w \cdot \pi(w)) = 0$, and $\pi$ is additive over concatenation.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[One flip flips the parity]\label{lem:flip}
For any $w$ and index $i$, flipping bit $i$ of $w$ negates $\pi(w)$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Every single-bit channel error is detected]
For every $x$ and every position $i$, $D^\pi$ applied to $E^\pi(x)$ with bit $i$ flipped returns $\bot$; it never returns a wrong message.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[The checksum is otherwise free]
$c^\pi$ is sound whenever $c$ is; $G(c^\pi) = G(c)$; $\fail_p(c^\pi) = \fail_p(c)$; and the length bound rises by exactly one.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Cost of the checksummed pipeline]
Parity verification costs one step per received bit, i.e. $k+2$ steps at rate $k$, and the enumerative decode a further $k+2$: exactly $2k+4$ steps in total.
\end{theorem}

\section{Block composition: parse-free concatenation and the union bound}

Let $c$ be fixed rate $\ell$ --- as the enumerative code is, with $\ell = k+1$, because the failure marker is padded to full length.

\begin{definition}[Block code]
The block encoder maps $v \in \A^n$ to $E(v_1)\cdots E(v_n)$. The block decoder slices the received word into $n$ chunks of $\ell$ bits, decodes each with $D$, and returns $\bot$ if any chunk fails or the length does not match.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The block decoder inverts the block encoder]
If every $v_i$ lies in $G(c)$, the block decoder returns $v$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$: since $|E(v_1)| = \ell$ exactly, the first $\ell$ bits are $E(v_1)$ and the remainder is the concatenation of the other codewords.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Soundness composes]
If $c$ is sound and fixed rate, the block code is sound for every $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Good sets multiply]\label{thm:goodprod}
For sound fixed-rate $c$, the good set of the block code is exactly $G(c)^n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{lemma}[Product mass]\label{lem:prodmass}
For $P(v) = \prod_{i=1}^n p(v_i)$ and $G \subseteq \A$, $P(G^n) = p(G)^n$; in particular $P$ is a probability distribution.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Union bound for block composition]\label{thm:union}
Let $c$ be sound and fixed rate with $\fail_p(c) \le \varepsilon$, $\varepsilon \ge 0$, $p \ge 0$. Then under the product source
\[
\fail_P(\text{block code}) = 1 - \big(1 - \fail_p(c)\big)^n \le n\varepsilon .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:goodprod} and Lemma~\ref{lem:prodmass} the good mass is $(1-\fail_p(c))^n \ge (1-\varepsilon)^n$. For $\varepsilon \le 1$, Bernoulli's inequality gives $(1-\varepsilon)^n \ge 1 - n\varepsilon$. For $\varepsilon > 1$ the bound is trivial when $n \ge 1$, and the failure probability is $0$ when $n = 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Exact block decoding complexity]
For the enumerative code at rate $k$ and $v \in S^n$, the instrumented block decoder returns $v$ at a cost of exactly $n(k+3)+1$ steps for $n(k+1)$ transmitted bits.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induction on $n$: each block costs one slicing step plus the $k+2$ steps of Theorem~\ref{thm:enumcost}; the base case costs $1$.
\end{proof}

A random codebook for a whole block of $n$ symbols has $2^{nk}$ entries, and the search bounds of the previous section apply to it verbatim.

\section{The master theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Scheme with full guarantee]
Let $S \subseteq \A$ with $|S| \le 2^k$ and $p(S) \ge 1-\varepsilon$. The checksummed enumerative code $\Enum(S,k)^\pi$ simultaneously satisfies:
\begin{enumerate}[label=\textup{(\arabic*)}]
\item \textbf{Soundness}: a returned message is always the true message;
\item \textbf{Rate}: every codeword has $k+2$ bits (index, failure flag, parity);
\item \textbf{Reliability}: $\fail_p \le \varepsilon$;
\item \textbf{Explicit failure reporting}: every $x \notin S$ decodes to $\bot$;
\item \textbf{Exact decoding complexity}: $2k+4$ steps on every $x \in S$;
\item \textbf{Channel error detection}: every single-bit corruption decodes to $\bot$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Matching converse]
Any sound code --- randomised or not --- with length bound $t$ and $\fail_p \le \varepsilon$ admits $S$ with $|S| + 1 \le 2^{t+1}$ and $p(S) \ge 1-\varepsilon$; and if $G(c) \ne \A$ then $|G(c)| + 2 \le 2^{t+1}$.
\end{theorem}

\section{Applications and algorithmic notes}

\textbf{Typical-set compression.} For an i.i.d.\ source of entropy $H$, the asymptotic equipartition property supplies typical sets of size $\approx 2^{nH}$ with mass $\ge 1-\varepsilon$; Theorem~\ref{thm:achiev} converts any such set into a code of rate $nH+1$ bits per block with detected failures, decodable in $nH+2$ steps. All statements here are non-asymptotic.

\textbf{Design rule.} Sort the alphabet by decreasing probability, take the shortest prefix $S$ with $p(S) \ge 1-\varepsilon$, set $k = \lceil \log_2 |S| \rceil$, and emit $k+2$ bits per symbol. By Theorem~\ref{thm:optimal} no sound scheme with the same $\varepsilon$ uses fewer than $\lceil \log_2(|S_{\min}|+2)\rceil - 1$ bits.

\textbf{Why fixed rate matters.} Variable-length codes need a parser, and parsing an unstructured variable-length codebook reintroduces search; fixed-rate codewords make block decoding a loop of slices.

\textbf{Storage.} The enumerative decoder stores the table $\iota^{-1}$ of size $|S|$, exactly as a random codebook must; random coding saves no memory, only design effort.

\textbf{Detected failures as a primitive.} A system may retransmit, fall back to a raw representation (costing $\varepsilon \log_2 N$ bits of expected overhead), or raise an alarm; what it never has to do is verify the output by other means.

\section{Open problems}

\begin{conjecture}[The union bound is the exact loss of block composition]
Theorem~\ref{thm:union} gives $\fail = 1 - (1-\varepsilon_0)^n \le n\varepsilon$. This is exactly optimal among all sound fixed-rate block schemes of total rate $n(k+1)$ on a product source: no joint code of the same rate has smaller failure probability, so joint typicality buys nothing beyond the per-block quantile.
\end{conjecture}

Applying Theorem~\ref{thm:optimal} to the product source reduces this to a rearrangement question: is a heaviest set of prescribed cardinality in a product measure always a product of per-coordinate heaviest sets? With a common marginal this is a statement about products of sorted vectors; the characterisation and the product-mass identity are in hand, and only the rearrangement step is open.

\begin{conjecture}[Query lower bound for unstructured codebooks]
Any decoder accessing a codebook only through equality probes $\cb(a) \stackrel{?}{=} w$ needs at least $|\text{codebook}|$ probes in the worst case, for every probe order and every adaptive strategy.
\end{conjecture}

The expected route is an adversary argument: while some message remains unprobed, the adversary may consistently declare it the true one.

Further directions: extend single-bit to $d$-bit error detection with a distance-$(d+1)$ code and recompute the exact rate and step counts; quantify the trade-off for hybrid schemes that fall back to raw transmission on $\bot$; and determine the optimal rate for list decoders of size $L$, where the counting bound should relax to $|S| \le L(2^{t+1}-1)$.

\section{Conclusion}

Relaxing exact decoding to $\varepsilon$-almost-lossless decoding does not repeal the pigeonhole bound; it re-indexes it by the $(1-\varepsilon)$-quantile of the source and charges exactly one codeword for the ability to announce failure. Within that region an explicit fixed-rate enumerative scheme is optimal to within two bits, decodes in $k+2$ steps ($2k+4$ with a single-bit-error-detecting checksum), composes over $n$ blocks parse-free with failure probability $1-(1-\varepsilon_0)^n \le n\varepsilon$ and decoding cost $n(k+3)+1$, and never corrupts silently. Monte Carlo codebooks provide no rate advantage --- the event that a random codebook succeeds is the counting condition the deterministic scheme is built from --- and charge an unbounded factor in decoding time. The resource that pays in almost-lossless compression is structure, not randomness.

